test_interpolate_compute -- interpolate and compute against independent
answers (user, Oct 20 2026: "add to the test suite some testing of
compute and interpolate"; "test keepatt with and without dudded
vertices; rmpoint/compress removes them")
=======================================================================

QUICK START

  run_interpolate_tests.sh                 28 decks, about 10 s
  open logs/demo.html                       what each method does (pictures)
  open logs/summary.html

RUN A DECK YOURSELF

  The input meshes are NOT stored in this folder: make_inputs.py
  writes them (fixed seeds, the same every time) into work/ at the
  start of each run, and each deck is copied there and run there.
  work/ is emptied when the next run starts. After a run:

    cd work
    ../../../../build/lagrit < ic25_continuous_hex_warped.lgi
    ls                                              # src_*.inp, sink_*.inp, out_*.inp

  Or make the inputs anywhere, without running the suite:

    python3 make_inputs.py ~/somewhere              # writes all *.inp there
    python3 demo/make_demo_inputs.py ~/somewhere    # the demo inputs

WHAT IT TESTS

  make_inputs.py writes small meshes into work/ (fixed seeds): 300
  random source points carrying fsrc = 1 + 2x - 3y + 0.5z, a 6 x 6 x 6
  tet mesh of the unit cube carrying the same linear field, the tets
  shrunk into [0.015, 0.985]^3, a 4 x 4 x 4 hex mesh with a cell value
  per hex, 400 random points inside the cube, and a plane of triangles
  at z = 0.4. check_ic.py computes every expected value itself (brute
  force nearest point, the linear formula, the hex containing each
  centroid) and compares within 1e-9.

  ic01  interpolate / voronoi
  ic02  voronoi, keepatt: stored lookup (pt_gtg) reused, then a fresh
        search; no dudded vertex
  ic03  ic02 with a dudded sink vertex (itp 21)        REGRESSION TEST
  ic04  ic02 after the dudded vertex is removed (rmpoint / compress)
  ic05  interpolate / continuous of a linear field (exact)
  ic06  continuous, keepatt: stored enclosing tets (el_gtg) reused
  ic07  ic06 with a dudded sink point
  ic08  interpolate / map of a cell attribute (hex -> tets)
  ic09  compute / distance_field
  ic10  ic09 with a dudded sink vertex                 REGRESSION TEST
  ic11  ic09 after rmpoint / compress
  ic12  compute / signed_distance_field to a plane: magnitude and a
        consistent sign on each side

  Every SOURCE cell type (user, Oct 20 2026: "the source should be tri,
  quad, prism, tet, and hex"; LaGriT splits quads into 4 triangles and
  prisms and hexes into tets, so a linear field must come out exact):
  ic13  continuous from triangles (2D)        ic17  map from triangles
  ic14  continuous from quads (2D)            ic18  map from quads
  ic15  continuous from prisms                ic19  map from prisms
  ic16  continuous from hexes                 ic20  map from tets
  ic21  continuous from the same hexes, numbered differently
  ic22  continuous onto points lying exactly on source edges
  ic23  continuous from triangles onto points on and just outside the
        exterior edges and corners (within 1e-14: the value; 1e-9 or
        more outside: the flag)
  ic24  the same from quads
  (ic22-ic24 need the inside_tri2d fix of Oct 21 2026; without it they
  FAIL: 109, 24 and 18 points get the flag value.)
  ic25  continuous from sheared, warped (non-planar faces) hexes,
        numbered turned over
  ic26  continuous from pyramids
  ic27  continuous from the prisms of ic15, numbered turned over
  ic28  a curved field from the warped hexes: interpolating from the
        hexes must equal hextotet / 24 then interpolating from the tets
  (ic15, ic21, ic25-ic28 need the median split in cinterpolate_elem of
  Oct 21 2026; without it ic15, ic21, ic25 and ic27 FAIL.)
  (ic05 is continuous from tets, ic08 map from hexes.)

  Dudded vertices are not judged (interpolate skips them by design).

  A deck with "# known:" lines is a documented failure: KNOWN when it
  fails as documented, FIXED (the run fails: update the deck) when it
  passes.

DEMONSTRATIONS (demo/, user Oct 20 2026: "a result that is intuitive
and visible to someone looking at the graphic")

  demo/demo.lgi runs five small cases and demo/make_demo_page.py draws
  them in logs/demo.html (not judged): voronoi cells of 25 points;
  continuous from a coarse triangle mesh beside the exact field;
  map from turned coarse cells onto fine cells; distance to points on
  a curve; signed distance to a sphere on a slice through it.

FOUND (Oct 20 2026, not fixed; for the user to decide)

  - FIXED Oct 21 2026 (inside_lg.f, inside_tri2d): sink points lying
    exactly on an edge shared by two source triangles were sometimes
    found in neither and got the flag value (bright streaks on the
    demo page; ic22: 109 of 10,201 grid points), and points within
    rounding outside an exterior edge were flagged (ic23, ic24). The
    edge test compared areas with a fraction of machine precision of
    the triangle size, smaller than the rounding in the cross
    products. inside_tri2d now accepts a point as on the triangle
    when no barycentric weight is below -tau, tau = 1000 machine
    epsilons times max(1, distance from origin / triangle size).

  - FIXED Oct 21 2026 (cinterpolate_elem.f; user's suggestion): the
    value for quads, pyramids, prisms and hexes did NOT come from a
    split into triangles / tets (those were only used by the inside
    test). Hexes and quads were split by the planes x=xp, y=yp (z=zp)
    through the point, assuming stored edge 1-2 along x, 2-3 along y,
    1-5 along z (as createpts / brick numbers it): any other numbering
    (ic21, quads rotated by one vertex) gave every value wrong (hexes
    about 1e14, "Zero Volume Found"); face-diagonal points were scaled
    by the distance from the ORIGIN. The prism (and pyramid) branch
    was a copy of the hex branch reading vertices 7 and 8 (ic15: up to
    106 off). Now each element is split around its median: quad into
    4 triangles, hex into the 24 tets of hextotet / 24 and inside_hex,
    prism into 14, pyramid into 8; a median carries the mean of its
    vertices. Linear fields are exact for any numbering and shape
    (ic15, ic21, ic25-ic27); a curved field equals hextotet / 24
    (ic28). Behaviour change: for axis-aligned bricks the old method
    was trilinear; for a curved field values move by about 0.1 x the
    interpolation error (test: max 0.004, mesh error 0.03).
    Note read / avs stores a hex's AVS vertices 1..8 as 5 6 7 8 1 2 3 4
    and a prism's 1..6 as 4 5 6 1 2 3 (readavs.f).
  - A source mesh with 0 cells (e.g. read / avs of an unknown cell
    keyword such as "pri": "Bad element type") makes interpolate /
    continuous crash (segmentation fault after "kdtree build -1" and a
    zero-length mmgetblk) instead of an ERROR.

  REGRESSION: ic03 and ic10 FAIL on LaGriT before the Oct 20 2026 fix
  of intrp_gtg.f (the stored lookup was read by list position instead
  of vertex number: 156 of 215 vertices wrong here; Jeffrey Hyman's
  DFM case, 1,936 of 8,521). All 12 PASS with the fix.

  compute / signed_distance_field (and distance_field) measure the
  distance to the nearest source VERTEX, not to the surface, as
  COMPUTE.md says ("the minimum distance from any node in mo_source").
  By design (user, Oct 20 2026): the true distance to a surface is much
  more complex; the source surface should be refined finely enough for
  the use. ic12 checks that definition.

FILES

  run_interpolate_tests.sh  runner (POSIX sh)
  make_inputs.py            input meshes (written into work/)
  check_ic.py               the checks; its opening comment defines them
  make_page.py              logs/summary.html
  demo/                     the demonstrations (logs/demo.html)
